MKDIR(2)                       P32W Unix                        MKDIR(2)

NAME
       mkdir - make a directory

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/stat.h>

       int mkdir(const char *path, int mode);

DESCRIPTION
       mkdir creates the directory path, holding the entries . and
       .., with the permission bits of mode (masked with 07777)
       less the bits set in the file creation mask (umask(2)). Its
       owner and group are the caller's effective user and group
       IDs. The parent directory must be writable; its link count
       goes up by one for the new directory's .. entry.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EEXIST  path exists (as a file or directory).
       ENOENT  A directory in path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path, or the parent directory is not writable.
       ENOSPC  No free inode, or no room for the entries.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       rmdir(2), chmod(2), umask(2), mkdir(1)

BUGS
       In V7 mkdir was a program built from mknod and link; here it
       is a system call, as in 4.2BSD.
